From 2967c409a7a6927597251a096fd0362ab5818a65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:26:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] leave the interface window visible under the stream
 window

Six of this boot's seven runs die inside the VeriSilicon EGL-Wayland
client the interface window draws through:

  opennow-qt: api/gc_egl_wayland.c:2699: buffer_fenced_release:
    Assertion `buffer->release_fence_fd == -1' failed.

The vendor client objects to recycling a swapchain image whose previous
release fence has not been answered yet. `0019`'s hide is what leaves a
fence unanswered: `QQuickWindow::hide()` is `QWaylandWindow::setVisible
(false)`, which runs `QWaylandWindow::reset()` and destroys the
interface's `wl_surface` while images are still in flight, so weston
never answers their releases and the images keep their pending fences.
The QPA window object and with it the EGL context survive the hide, so
the next recycle after it hits the assert: 33 ms and 35 ms after
`opennow-interface-shown` in `ab81` and `ab82`, about a session end
later in `ab80` and `ab83`, in the seat's artwork reload. Every run
that died logged `opennow-interface-hidden`; the 535 s run that never
left the streaming state logged zero asserts.

The deaths also feed the DMA pool. `CmaFree` read with nothing running
went 287 MB to 232 MB to 126 MB across the boot, and dmesg's
`cma_alloc: reserved: alloc failed, req-size: 893 pages` asks for 3.5
MB, one 1080p NV12 set, so each crash costs the pool about 55 MB, and
after a few of them the next session fails at the export of the
companion patch instead of here. Only a reboot returns the 512 MB pool.

This reverts the hide: the interface window keeps painting under the
stream window, so every fence is answered and the swapchain is never
poisoned. That is the state `0014`'s swap interval 2 was measured in
already - the stream held 58.6-60 fps with the interface painting at
30 Hz - and the log says so for the whole session: `opennow-present
fps=`/`render=` counts the interface's work and `wayland-video fps=`
counts the stream's, in the same run, beside the load average.

Greppable markers: opennow-interface-visible, opennow-present, wayland-video fps
---
 opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp | 31 +++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp b/opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp
index a28a03e..598a2d2 100644
--- a/opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp
+++ b/opennow-qt/src/app/ApplicationStartup.cpp
@@ -587,17 +587,26 @@ static int runApplicationSession(int argc, char *argv[], QString &restartExecuta
         auto *videoWindow = WaylandVideoSurface::instance();
         QObject::connect(
             videoWindow, &WaylandVideoSurface::mappedChanged, rootWindow,
-            [rootWindow, videoWindow, saved = QWindow::Windowed]() mutable {
-                if (videoWindow->isMapped()) {
-                    if (!rootWindow->isVisible()) return;
-                    saved = rootWindow->visibility();
-                    qInfo("opennow-interface-hidden: the stream window has a frame");
-                    rootWindow->hide();
-                    return;
-                }
-                if (rootWindow->isVisible()) return;
-                qInfo("opennow-interface-shown: the stream window is gone");
-                rootWindow->setVisibility(saved);
+            [rootWindow, videoWindow, first = true]() mutable {
+                // 0019 hid the interface window the moment the stream window
+                // had a frame, and hiding is what poisons the interface's
+                // EGL client: setVisible(false) runs QWaylandWindow::reset(),
+                // which destroys the window's wl_surface while swapchain
+                // images are still in flight, so their release fences are
+                // never answered and the next recycle of one of them is
+                //   opennow-qt: api/gc_egl_wayland.c:2699: buffer_fenced_release:
+                //   Assertion `buffer->release_fence_fd == -1' failed.
+                // Six of the seven runs of this boot died exactly that way,
+                // 33 ms and 35 ms after opennow-interface-shown in two of
+                // them, and each of those deaths costs the 512 MB DMA pool
+                // about 55 MB, which is what then makes the next session
+                // fail at export instead. Leaving the window visible keeps
+                // every fence answered, which is the state 0014's swap
+                // interval 2 was measured in: the stream held 58.6-60 fps
+                // with the interface painting at 30 Hz.
+                if (!videoWindow->isMapped() || !first) return;
+                first = false;
+                qInfo("opennow-interface-visible: the interface keeps painting under the stream window");
             });
     }
 #endif
